ngles (6, 9, 10, K, 2) and one strong pair (AA). With no triplets or sequences, the strategy relies on clearing trash singles while retaining control cards (2, AA) to regain the lead. Playing the pair of Aces now would consume a high-value control hand on a free turn, leaving the player vulnerable to opponents\' pair leads. Playing the lowest single (♥6) forces opponents to spend cards to win the trick, and allows the player to keep the AA as a strong stopper against pair leads and the 2 as a stopper for single leads. This defensive setup maximizes the utility of the high cards.\n\nReason: Playing the lowest single (♥6) is the optimal move to offload a weak card while preserving the pair of Aces (♠A ♣A) and the single 2 (♥2) as control cards. Using the Aces now would strip the hand of its only defense against pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥6"]}'}
